.TH VOICEWMTEST 1
.SH NAME
voicewmtest \- what a real window announces when it takes the keyboard
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B voicewmtest
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Voicewmtest
runs under
.IR wm (1),
makes a real client window with a titlebar, and reads
.B /dev/voice/history
to see what the whole stack said when the window manager handed that
window the keyboard.
It is in
.IR runtests (1)'s
graphical list, and needs
.B -g
and a window context.
.PP
It checks one thing: that a window entry names the window
.IR once .
.SS Why it is separate from tkofftest
.IR Tkofftest (1)
builds its toplevels with
.B tk->toplevel
and hands them an image of its own, which is what lets it read pixels
back at all.
That path never goes through
.IR wmclient (2),
and two layers meeting is exactly where an announcement can go wrong.
.PP
For a while a window entry said its name twice, once from
.I wmclient
writing the title itself and once from
.BR libtk ;
nothing offscreen could have seen it.
.SS What it catches, and what it does not
Checked by putting the bug back: two layers announcing the same thing in
.I different
words is caught \(em it reports
.B "named 2 times"
and prints both.
.PP
Two layers announcing the same words
.I exactly
is not caught, because
.IR voice (3)
drops an announcement identical to the one before it, so that case is
already harmless.
That is a property of the device rather than a hole here, but it is worth
knowing before trusting this to settle a layering question.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/voicewmtest.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR runtests (1),
.IR tkofftest (1),
.IR voice (3),
.IR wmclient (2)
.SH BUGS
One window, one event.
It says nothing about what is announced while a window is used \(em
moving between controls, walking a menu, moving about in text \(em all of
which
.IR tkofftest (1)
checks offscreen and nothing checks here.
.PP
The history is not clearable, so this counts the lines before and after
rather than starting from nothing. A session that announced a great deal
before it ran would still work, but the counting is the fragile part.
